technical_analysis:
## Root cause

`MainActivity` must be exported to serve as the launcher entry point, but the same component also declares share-target intent filters and carries no guarding permission, so the share channel is reachable by any third-party app:

```xml title=android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml startLineNumber=57 endLineNumber=92
<activity
    android:name=".MainActivity"
    android:exported="true"
    android:launchMode="singleTop"
    ...
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND"/>
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
        <data android:mimeType="text/*"/>
    </intent-filter>
```

The manifest additionally declares `SEND` for `image/*` and `*/*` and three matching `SEND_MULTIPLE` filters (lines 83-108). `MainActivity` has no `android:permission` attribute, and no caller-identity check exists anywhere in the Android sources — a repository-wide search for `getCallingActivity`, `getCallingPackage`, `Binder.getCallingUid`, `checkCallingPermission` and `getReferrer` returns no matches. Explicit intents do not need to match an intent filter at all, so the attacker does not even depend on the filter declarations; they are cited because they establish the component as a legitimate share target.

## Delivery into the app

The `receive_sharing_intent` plugin (1.9.0, `pubspec.yaml:56`, resolved at `pubspec.lock:1402-1409`) reads the shared text and emits it verbatim as the JSON `path` field:

```kotlin title=android/src/main/kotlin/com/kasem/receive_sharing_intent/ReceiveSharingIntentPlugin.kt startLineNumber=120 endLineNumber=124
Intent.ACTION_SEND -> {
    val uri = intent.parcelable<Uri>(Intent.EXTRA_STREAM)
    val text = intent.getStringExtra(Intent.EXTRA_TEXT)
    toJsonObject(uri, text, intent.type)?.let { JSONArray(listOf(it)) }
}
```

Both lifecycle paths feed the Dart layer: `onAttachedToActivity` handles the cold-start intent (`:186-190`) and `onNewIntent` the live one (`:205-208`), the latter registered as a `NewIntentListener` at `:188`. The plugin is registered in `android/app/src/main/java/io/flutter/plugins/GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java:129`.

The Dart side maps the payload without inspection and broadcasts it:

```dart title=lib/domain/services/share/share_target_service.dart startLineNumber=105 endLineNumber=114
SharedPayload? mapFile(SharedMediaFile file) {
  if (file.path.trim().isEmpty) return null;
  return SharedPayload(
    kind: _mapKind(file.type),
    value: file.path,
    ...
```

`ShareTargetCubit._onPayloads` (`share_target_cubit.dart:42-48`) emits `ShareTargetReceived`, and `ChatScreen` reacts to that state by consuming the payloads and submitting them — with no dialog, no prompt, and no user action:

```dart title=lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart startLineNumber=390 endLineNumber=397
_shareTargetSub = shareCubit.stream.listen((state) {
  if (state is ShareTargetReceived && mounted) {
    final payloads = shareCubit.consume();
    if (payloads.isNotEmpty) {
      unawaited(_submitSharedPayloads(payloads));
    }
  }
});
```

`ChatScreen` is mounted in production: `app_router.dart:28` starts at `/splash`, `splash_screen.dart:66-108` navigates to `/home`, and `main_navigation_shell.dart:34-42` builds `ChatScreen()` as the first tab of an `IndexedStack`, so the subscription and the cold-start drain are live without any user interaction.

## Sink

`_submitSharedPayloads` builds a prompt from the attacker text and, if a URL is present, fetches it before submitting:

```dart title=lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart startLineNumber=536 endLineNumber=542
final reader = _resolveUrlReader();
final url = payloads
    .map((p) =>
        p.isUrl ? p.value : (UrlReaderServiceBase.extractUrl(p.value) ?? ''))
    .firstWhere((u) => u.isNotEmpty, orElse: () => '');
if (reader != null && url.isNotEmpty) {
  final result = await reader.fetchReadableText(url);
```

and then submits unconditionally:

```dart title=lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart startLineNumber=563 endLineNumber=564
if (!mounted) return;
_handleSubmitted(context, prompt);
```

`_handleSubmitted` reaches the agent with no confirmation gate:

```dart title=lib/presentation/screen/chat/chat_screen.dart startLineNumber=1501 endLineNumber=1505
if (_mode == ChatMode.direct) {
  final attachments = _stagedAttachment == null
      ? const <ChatAttachment>[]
      : <ChatAttachment>[_stagedAttachment!];
  context.read<AgentCubit>().sendMessage(prompt, attachments: attachments);
```

(`_mode` defaults to `ChatMode.direct` via the constructor default at `chat_screen.dart:76`; in gateway mode the equivalent sink is `client.sendChatMessage(prompt)` at `:1522`.) `sendMessage` (`agent_cubit.dart:502-536`) appends the text as a `role: 'user'` message and enters the ReAct loop at `:535`.

## The fetch control

```dart title=lib/domain/tools/chat/url_reader_service.dart startLineNumber=59 endLineNumber=70
static String? validateUrl(String url) {
  final trimmed = url.trim();
  if (trimmed.isEmpty) return 'URL is empty';
  final uri = Uri.tryParse(trimmed);
  if (uri == null) return 'URL is missing a host';
  final scheme = uri.scheme.toLowerCase();
  if (scheme != 'http' && scheme != 'https') {
    return 'Unsupported URL scheme: ${uri.scheme}';
  }
  if (uri.host.isEmpty) return 'URL is missing a host';
  return null;
}
```

This is the only check on the destination. The fetch itself is a `Dio` GET with `followRedirects: true` (`lib/data/datasources/tools/chat/url_reader_service.dart:43-54`) using the app-wide `Dio` singleton (`lib/common/injection/modules.dart:13-17`, no interceptors or proxy). `extractUrl` (`lib/domain/tools/chat/url_reader_service.dart:10-37`) additionally accepts bare domains and is applied to arbitrary shared text at `chat_screen.dart:537-540`, so the attacker does not need a full URL literal.

## CWE selection

Recorded as **CWE-940 (Improper Verification of Source of a Communication Channel)**. The defect is that the app consumes an IPC message from an unauthenticated, unverified sender and acts on its content as though it were user input; the missing authorization is a consequence of that missing source verification, and CWE-862 would describe the broader exported-component family already recorded separately for the action-extra channel. CWE-940 names the specific broken control here.

## Controls examined and found absent

- No confirmation dialog, user tap, settings toggle, feature flag or consent gate anywhere on the intent → `sendMessage` path (repository-wide search for `confirm` / `consent` / `autoSubmit` and for any share-related preference key returned nothing; the only settings keys in `settings_keys` concern LLM, gateway, geofence, overlay, NFC, TTS and ambient settings).
- No allowlist, sanitisation or provenance marking applied to the shared text. A `[LINK CONTEXT]` prefix exists (`shared_payload.dart:55`) but is applied only to the fetched link block at `:74-81`; the attacker's raw text remains present unmarked in the same prompt, and for the non-URL cases (`text`, `file`, `image`, `video`) no marker is applied at all (`toChatPrompt`, `shared_payload.dart:36-49`).
- No caller verification in the Android layer (searches listed above).
- No destination restriction on the fetch beyond scheme and host presence.

## Distinctness from the action-extra channel

This is a separate attacker-controlled input and a separate consumer from the `extra_flames_action` path recorded elsewhere for this component. `MainActivity.extractActionFromIntent` (`MainActivity.kt:73-85`) reads only `extra_flames_action`; the share payload never passes through it. Here the input is share-sheet content (`EXTRA_TEXT` / `EXTRA_STREAM`) and the consumer is the share-target pipeline terminating in `chat_screen.dart`. The two share the same root exported-component weakness but are independently fixable — an allowlist on the action extra does nothing for the share text, and the Dart-side fix proposed here does nothing for the action extra.

